Q: React Leaflet Error with HMR - Cannot read properties of undefined A: <p>This isn’t about the DOM being “not ready”. It’s an issue with Leaflet + React</p>
<p>Leaflet mutates the DOM imperatively, and during HMR, React can replace the component while Leaflet still holds stale DOM references, causing <code>appendChild</code> on <code>undefined</code>. This only happens in development, there’s no Fast Refresh in production.</p>
<p>A simple dev-only workaround is to force a full Leaflet remount by changing the <code>key</code> on <code>MapContainer</code>:</p>
<pre><code>const [mapKey, setMapKey] = useState(0);

useEffect(() =&gt; {
  setMapKey(k =&gt; k + 1);
}, []);

&lt;MapContainer key={mapKey} ...&gt;
  ...
&lt;/MapContainer&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>This forces a new Leaflet instance after a hot update and avoids the stale DOM references.</p>
<p>I wouldn’t worry about this affecting production. If it does, that usually points to a different problem (conditional layers, version mismatch, missing cleanup), not HMR.</p>

Q: Interceptor not intercepting in Angular 17 A: <h3>My story and how I +AI solved it.</h3>
<h5>SPECIALLY For those migrating from Angular 14 -&gt; 17, starting using .NET WebApi with SPA Angular template --- Angular has different startup configuration and even different startup file sets, mine uses <code>angular.json</code> and <code>main.ts</code> and <code>index.html</code></h5>
<p>I migrated from angular 14 to 17 sometime ago, and I wished to use functional style for moving toward future,... most of pieces kept working, until I came across the functional interceptor one... but as the almighty AI describe it, and no matter how I asked, the answer were one thing, I either have to revert to class base, or adapt to new system, so I again moved toward future, knowing (wrongly) that I need to remove at minimum the interceptors rewriting them all, which one of them is way to complex, but none of the issue arise at the end, and it was a happy ending, both OLD system and new system kept working altogether...</p>
<p>Note: I didn't test all parts of the system, but I did a minimum check, including whether old and new interceptors are involved. I just wish to report back to this page as soon as possible, so this experience may come in handy, whether a human reads it or an AI.</p>
<h4>What I have and What I want to accomplish</h4>
<p>Okay... let go with what I had and what I wanted to accomplish, which is the misunderstanding point, or the point where post and topic need to clarify what they want and what they gain, otherwise incorrect use may not be beneficial.</p>
<p>Up until now, I had these kinds of interceptors, the class based one:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>@Injectable()
export class AuthInterceptor implements HttpInterceptor {
  private isRefreshing = false;
  private refreshTokenSubject = new BehaviorSubject&lt;string | null&gt;(null);
</code></pre>
<p>And I wanted to create a new interceptor using a function-based style, like:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>export const globalErrorInterceptor: HttpInterceptorFn = (req, next) =&gt; {
  const notification = inject(NotificationService);
  const globalErrorHandler = inject(GlobalErrorHandlerLogic)
</code></pre>
<h3>What I tried:</h3>
<p>I tried to register my interceptor withing the existing architecture of the system in <code>CoreModule</code> which in turn it get cascaded in LayoutModule, and some other modules</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    AccessIfDirective,
    AccessEnableDirective
  ],
  exports: [
    AccessIfDirective,
    AccessEnableDirective,
  ],
  providers: [
    PermissionProviderService,
    { provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS, useClass: XsrfInterceptor, multi: true },
    { provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS, useClass: AuthInterceptor, multi: true },
    // **** THIS LINE ****
    { provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS, useValue: globalErrorInterceptor, multi: true }
  ]
})
export class CoreModule {
  constructor(@Optional() @SkipSelf() parent?: CoreModule) {
    if (parent) throw new Error('CoreModule is already loaded. Import only in AppModule.');
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>as you can see it uses <code>useValue</code> instead of <code>useClass</code> no error so far, but the interceptor wouldn't get called.</p>
<p>Next I found this, which re-register one of the interceptors... unlike the other place, it made all HTTP broken, and caused code failure...</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    AppComponent,
    CounterComponent,
  ],
  imports: [
    BrowserModule.withServerTransition({appId: 'ng-cli-universal'}),
    HttpClientModule,
    FormsModule,
    AppRoutingModule,
    BrowserAnimationsModule,
    AgGridModule,
    MaterialFeatureModule,
    SharedModule,
    MatSnackBarModule,
    DialogsModule,
  ],
  exports: [
   // ControlMessagesComponent,
  ],
  providers: [
    AppConfig,
    {
      provide: APP_INITIALIZER,
      useFactory: appInitFactory,
      deps: [AppConfig, PermissionProviderService],
      multi: true
    },

    BaseApiService,
    {
      provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS,
      useClass: AuthInterceptor,
      multi: true
    },
// **** HERE ****
    {
      provide: HTTP_INTERCEPTORS,
      useValue: globalErrorInterceptor,
      multi: true
    }
    //{ provide: Directionality, useValue: { value: 'rtl' } }
    // { provide: LOCALE_ID, useValue: 'fa' },
  ],,
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule { }
</code></pre>
<h3>Working solution</h3>
<p>In the end the solution for me was to modify a sets of files:</p>
<ol>
<li>Updating the main.ts to use <code>bootstrapApplication</code> instead of <code>platformBrowserDynamic</code>:</li>
</ol>
<p>in <code>main.ts</code>:</p>
<pre><code>/// &lt;reference types=&quot;@angular/localize&quot; /&gt;

import {enableProdMode, importProvidersFrom} from '@angular/core';
import { platformBrowserDynamic } from '@angular/platform-browser-dynamic';

import { AppModule } from './app/app.module';
import { environment } from './environments/environment';
import {bootstrapApplication} from &quot;@angular/platform-browser&quot;;
import {AppComponent} from &quot;./app/app.component&quot;;
import {GlobalErrorHandlerLogic} from &quot;./app/core/errors/global-error-handler.logic&quot;;
import {NotificationService} from &quot;./app/services/notification.service&quot;;
import {provideHttpClient, withInterceptors} from &quot;@angular/common/http&quot;;
import {globalErrorInterceptor} from &quot;./app/core/interceptors/global-error.interceptor&quot;;

if (environment.production) {
  enableProdMode();
}

// BOOTSTRAP
// **** OLD CONFIG: ****
// platformBrowserDynamic().bootstrapModule(AppModule)
//   .catch(err =&gt; console.log(err));

// **** NEW CONFIG ****
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    // ⬅ KEEP ALL YOUR OLD PROVIDERS/MODULES
    importProvidersFrom(AppModule), &lt;--- APP MODULE THAT EXISTED, all old moduled configurations, can still work by wiring it through here

    // * Services i used in my interceptor, AI suggest to register them, but as you see I commented them
    // // old module-based providers that are compatible can stay
    // NotificationService,
    // GlobalErrorHandlerLogic,

    // modern functional HTTP client + interceptors
    provideHttpClient( // &lt;------- interceptors goes inside HttpClient configurations
      withInterceptors([ // &lt;------ This should wrap the interceptor
        globalErrorInterceptor // &lt;------- My interceptor
        // you can add more functional interceptors here
      ])
    ),
  ],
})
  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(err));
</code></pre>
<p>Note <code>AppComponent</code> is the very first component that my template uses in the <code>index.html</code> file</p>
<ol start="2">
<li>Make the AppComponent singletone (your top level router outlet holder):</li>
</ol>
<p>Note that whatever the component used before and you previously registered in your module (app.module.ts), now you need to directly import in your component, or any other standalone component you declare... (There is a way to declare them globally as well for example regarding commonly used UI modules... I expline below everything)</p>
<p>in <code>app.component.ts</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  standalone: true, // -----------&gt; ADD THIS, otherwise the bootstrap start nagging
  imports: [ // ----------&gt; import the routerOutlet or any other routing or other component that you used in your top-level component, for me RouterOutlet is the only one
    RouterOutlet
  ]
})
export class AppComponent {
  title = 'app';
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>RouterOutlet</code> add support for this tag: <code>&lt;router-outlet&gt;&lt;/router-outlet&gt;</code></p>
<ol start="3">
<li>in <code>app.module.ts</code>:</li>
</ol>
<p>remove the <code>AppComponent</code> references from <code>declarations</code> and <code>bootstrap</code> sections... and probably your imports in top of file.</p>
<h3>Module Importing tip for new users to <code>standalone</code> components</h3>
<p>regarding what i said about you can import module once, and use many time for standalone components... I still didn't try it, but come across this code and kept it for later:</p>
<p>in <code>main.ts</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>import { GlobalMaterialModule } from './app/core/material.providers';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    importProvidersFrom(AppModule),
    importProvidersFrom(GlobalMaterialModule),
    provideHttpClient(withInterceptors([globalErrorInterceptor]))
  ]
});
</code></pre>
<p>material.providers.ts -- custom module file</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import {
  MatButtonModule,
  MatCardModule,
  MatDialogModule,
  MatIconModule,
  MatInputModule,
  MatToolbarModule,
  // ...all other Material modules you use
} from '@angular/material';

@NgModule({
  exports: [
    MatButtonModule,
    MatCardModule,
    MatDialogModule,
    MatIconModule,
    MatInputModule,
    MatToolbarModule,
    // ...others
  ]
})
export class GlobalMaterialModule {}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Also @William Hou seem to have another method for registration, which the images didn't loaded for me initially due to my country <code>Iran</code> net blocking protocol  in these days, which recently start giving limited access, which the mentioned methods worth giving a try to automate import from angular DI (I'm not sure if DI registration is auto wired, or manual)</p>

Q: Screen reader for file upload A: <p>You can’t make a screen reader “react” to an <code>onChange</code> event itself.<br />
Screen readers announce changes in accessible DOM, not JavaScript events.</p>
<p>For <code>&lt;input type=&quot;file&quot;&gt;</code>, browsers do not reliably announce which files were added or removed, so you must expose that change as text in the DOM.</p>
<h3>What you need to do</h3>
<ol>
<li><p>Listen to the file input’s <code>onChange</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>Update some visible text in the DOM that represents the new state</p>
</li>
<li><p>Put that text inside an <code>aria-live</code> region, so the screen reader announces it</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h3>Minimal React example</h3>
<pre><code>import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;

function FileInputA11y() {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState(&quot;&quot;);

  function onChange(e: React.ChangeEvent&lt;HTMLInputElement&gt;) {
    const files = e.target.files;
    if (!files || files.length === 0) {
      setMessage(&quot;No files selected&quot;);
    } else {
      setMessage(
        `${files.length} file${files.length &gt; 1 ? &quot;s&quot; : &quot;&quot;} selected`
      );
    }
  }

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;input type=&quot;file&quot; multiple onChange={onChange} /&gt;

      &lt;div aria-live=&quot;polite&quot; role=&quot;status&quot;&gt;
        {message}
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>When the file selection changes, the text in the <code>aria-live</code> region updates and the screen reader announces it.</p>
<h3>About added / removed files</h3>
<p>If you need announcements like <em>“Added: a.pdf”</em> or <em>“Removed: b.png”</em>, keep the previous file list in state and diff it inside <code>onChange</code>, then update the <code>aria-live</code> text accordingly. The core idea is the same: state change -&gt; DOM text change -&gt; screen reader announcement.</p>
<p><em>Optional</em>: I made a small hook <code>useAnnouncer</code> to simplify announcing events:<br />
<a href="https://github.com/cuteshaun/react-aria-hooks" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/cuteshaun/react-aria-hooks</a></p>
